<article class="env-lemma" id="0F1Z">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0F1Z">Lemma <span data-tag="0F1Z">110.12.6</span>.</a> If $R$ is a valuation ring of dimension $> 1$, then $R[[x]]$ is flat over $R$ but not flat over $R[x]$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The arguments above show that this is true if we can show that $R$ is not completely normal (valuation rings are normal, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00IC" data-tag="00IC">10.50.3</a>). Let $\mathfrak p \subset \mathfrak m \subset R$ be a chain of primes. Pick nonzero $x \in \mathfrak p$ and $y \in \mathfrak m \setminus \mathfrak p$. Then $x y^{-n} \in R$ for all $n \geq 1$ (if not then $y^ n/x \in R$ which is absurd because $y \not\in \mathfrak p$). Hence $1/y$ is almost integral over $R$ but not in $R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>